== Chapter X

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The #smallcaps[second] particular direction: Get a clear sense of — 1. The guilt of the perplexing sin — Considerations proposed to help in this — 2. Its many dangers — (1.) Hardening — (2.) Temporal correction — (3.) Loss of peace and strength — (4.) Eternal destruction — Rules for handling this consideration — 3. Its evil — (1.) In grieving the Spirit — (2.) Wounding the new creature — \[(3.) Taking away a man’s usefulness.\]]

==== Second Direction: A Clear Sense of the Guilt, Danger, and Evil of the Sin

#smallcaps[The second] direction is this: #emph[Get a clear and lasting sense on your mind and conscience of the guilt, danger, and evil] of the sin that perplexes you:

1\. Of its #emph[guilt]. It is one of the deceits of a prevailing lust to make light of its own guilt. “Is it not a small one?” “When I bow down in the temple of Rimmon, may the #smallcaps[Lord] forgive your servant in this matter.” “Though this is bad, it is not as bad as such and such an evil. Others of the people of God have been in such a frame. Yes, what dreadful sins in act some of them have fallen into!” There are countless ways in which sin turns the mind away from a right and proper grasp of its guilt. Its foul vapors darken the mind, so that it cannot judge things rightly. Perplexing reasonings, promises that make light of sin, raging desires, treacherous resolutions to give it up, hopes of mercy: all have their share in disturbing the mind as it considers the guilt of a prevailing lust. The prophet tells us that when lust comes to its height it will do this completely: “Promiscuity, wine, and new wine take away the heart” (Hos 4:11), the heart, that is, the understanding, as the word is often used in Scripture. And as these things accomplish this work to the full in unregenerate persons, so they do it in part in the regenerate also. Solomon tells you of the man who was enticed by the immoral woman that he was “among the simple”; he was “a young man lacking judgment” (Prov 7:7). And where did his folly show itself? Why, he says in verse 23, he went on “not knowing it will cost him his life.” He did not consider the guilt of the evil he was caught up in. And when the Lord gives a reason why his dealings with Ephraim had no better effect, he gives this account: “Ephraim has become like a silly, senseless dove” (Hos 7:11); he had no understanding of his own miserable condition. Would it have been possible for David to lie so long in the guilt of that abominable sin, if he had not had countless corrupt reasonings keeping him from taking a clear view of its ugliness and guilt in the mirror of the law? That is why the prophet who was sent to awaken him, in his dealings with him, shut off every loophole and pretense by his parable, so that David might come fully under a sense of its guilt. This is the natural result of lust in the heart: it darkens the mind so that it will not judge its guilt rightly. And it has many other ways of making light of itself that I will not dwell on now.

So let this be the first care of the man who would mortify sin: to fix a right judgment of its guilt in his mind. To that end, take these considerations to help you:

(1.) In those who have #emph[inherent grace], the power of sin is weakened by it, so that sin will not have dominion over them as it has over others. Yet #emph[the guilt of the sin that still remains and abides is made heavier and greater by that grace]: “What then shall we say? Shall we continue in sin so that grace may increase? Certainly not! How can #emph[we] who died to sin live in it any longer?” (Rom 6:1, 2). “How can we who died?” The emphasis is on the word “we.” How can #emph[we] do it, who, as he describes it afterward, have received grace from Christ to the contrary? If we do it, we are surely more evil than any. I will not dwell on what especially makes the sins of such persons heavier: how they sin against more love, mercy, grace, help, relief, means, and deliverances than others. But let this consideration stay in your mind: there is inconceivably more evil and guilt in the evil that remains in your heart than there would be in as much sin if you had no grace at all. Observe:

(2.) God sees a great deal of beauty and excellence in the desires of his servants’ hearts, more than in the most glorious works of other men, yes, more than in most of their own outward performances, which have a greater mixture of sin than the desires and longings of grace in the heart have. In the same way, God sees #emph[a great deal of evil in the working of lust in their hearts, yes, more than in the open, notorious acts of wicked men], or in many outward sins the saints may fall into, since more opposition is made against those, and more humbling generally follows them. So Christ, dealing with his declining children, goes to the root with them and sets their profession aside: “I know your deeds” (Rev 3:15). “You are something quite different from what you profess, and this makes you abominable.”

So let these things, and considerations like them, bring you to a clear sense of the guilt of your indwelling lust, so that there may be no room in your heart for thoughts that make light of it or excuse it, through which sin will gain strength and prevail before you know it.

2\. Consider its #emph[danger], which is of many kinds:

(1.) The danger of being #emph[hardened by its deceitfulness]. The apostle presses this hard on the Hebrews: “See to it, brothers, that none of you#super[+] has a wicked heart of unbelief that turns away from the living God. But exhort one another daily, as long as it is called today, so that none of you#super[+] may be hardened by sin’s deceitfulness” (Heb 3:12, 13). “Take heed,” he says; “use every means, consider your#super[+] temptations, watch diligently. There is a treachery, a deceit, in sin that tends to harden your#super[+] hearts from the fear of God.” The hardening mentioned here goes to the utmost, to utter hardness of heart. Sin tends toward it, and every disorder and lust will make at least some progress toward it. You who were tender, who used to melt under the word and under afflictions, will grow, as some have profanely put it, “sermon-proof and sickness-proof.” You who used to tremble at the presence of God, at thoughts of death and of appearing before him, when you had more assurance of his love than you have now, will have a hardness on your spirit that these things cannot move. Your soul and your sin will be spoken of and spoken to, and you will not be concerned at all. You will be able to go through duties, praying, hearing, and reading, and your heart not be affected in the least. Sin will grow #emph[a light thing] to you; you will pass it by as a thing of nothing. This is what it will grow to. And what will be the end of such a condition? Can anything sadder befall you? Is it not enough to make any heart tremble, to think of being brought into a state where he has slight thoughts of sin? Slight thoughts of grace, of mercy, of the blood of Christ, of the law, heaven, and hell, all come in at the same time. Take heed: this is what your lust is working toward, the hardening of the heart, the searing of the conscience, the blinding of the mind, the numbing of the affections, and the deceiving of the whole soul.

(2.) The danger of some great #emph[temporal correction], which the Scripture calls “vengeance,” “judgment,” and “punishment” (Ps 89:30–33). Though God should not utterly cast you off for this abomination that lies in your heart, yet he will visit you with the rod. Though he pardons and forgives, he will take vengeance on your misdeeds. Oh, remember David and all his troubles! Look at him fleeing into the wilderness, and consider the hand of God on him. Is it nothing to you that God should kill your child in anger, ruin your estate in anger, break your bones in anger, let you become a scandal and a reproach in anger, kill you, destroy you, make you lie down in darkness, in anger? Is it nothing that he should punish, ruin, and undo others for your sake? Do not mistake me. I do not mean that God always sends all these things on #emph[his own] in anger; God forbid! But this I say: when he does deal with you in this way, and your conscience bears witness with him to what your provocations have been, you will find his dealings full of bitterness to your soul. If #emph[you do not fear] these things, #emph[I fear] you are under hardness.

(3.) #emph[Loss of peace and strength] all a man’s days. To have peace with God, and to have strength to walk before God, is the sum of the great promises of the covenant of grace. In these things lies the life of our souls. Without them in some comforting measure, to live is to die. What good will our lives do us if we do not sometimes see the face of God in peace, if we do not have some strength to walk with him? Now an unmortified lust will certainly rob men’s souls of both of these. The case is so plain in David that nothing could be clearer. How often he complains on this account that his bones were broken, his soul disquieted, his wounds grievous! Take other instances: “I was enraged by his sinful greed, so I struck him and hid My face in anger” (Isa 57:17). What peace, I ask, does a soul have while God hides himself, or what strength while he strikes? “I will return to My place until they admit their guilt and seek My face” (Hos 5:15). “I will leave them and hide my face, and what will become of their peace and strength?” So if you have ever enjoyed peace with God, if his terrors have ever made you afraid, if you have ever had strength to walk with him, or have ever mourned in your prayer and been troubled because of your weakness, think of this danger that hangs over your head. Perhaps in only a little while you will see the face of God in peace no more. Perhaps by tomorrow you will not be able to pray, read, hear, or perform any duty with the least cheerfulness, life, or vigor. And perhaps you may never see a quiet hour as long as you live, but may carry broken bones about with you, full of pain and terror, all the days of your life. Yes, perhaps God will shoot his arrows at you and fill you with anguish and disquiet, with fears and perplexities. He may make you a terror and an astonishment to yourself and others, show you hell and wrath every moment, and frighten and scare you with sad thoughts of his hatred, so that your sore will run in the night season and your soul will refuse comfort. You may wish for death rather than life; yes, your soul may choose strangling. Consider this a little. Though God should not utterly destroy you, he might still cast you into this condition, where you will have quick and living fears of your destruction. Accustom your heart to thoughts of this; let it know what is likely to be the outcome of its state. Do not leave this consideration until you have made your soul tremble within you.

(4.) There is the danger of #emph[eternal destruction].

To handle this consideration properly, observe:

\[1.\] There is such a #emph[connection] between #emph[continuing in sin] and #emph[eternal destruction] that, though God does resolve to deliver some from continuing in sin so that they may not be destroyed, he will deliver none from destruction who continue in sin. So as long as any man lies under the abiding power of sin, the threats of destruction and of everlasting separation from God are to be held out to him. So Heb 3:12; add to it Heb 10:38. This is the rule of God’s proceeding: if any man “turns away” from him, “shrinks back” through unbelief, “I will take no pleasure in him”; that is, “my indignation will pursue him to destruction.” So it is plainly in Gal 6:8.

\[2.\] The man who is #emph[so entangled] under the power of any corruption, as described above, can have at that time no clear, prevailing evidence of his interest in the covenant, by whose power he might be delivered from the fear of destruction. So destruction from the Lord may rightly be a terror to him, and he may, and ought to, look on it as #emph[the end of his course and ways]. “There is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus” (Rom 8:1). True; but who will have the comfort of this statement? Who may claim it for himself? “Those who walk according to the Spirit, and not according to the flesh.” But you will say, “Is this not persuading men to unbelief?” I answer, no. There are two judgments a man may make of himself: first, of his #emph[person]\; and, secondly, of his #emph[ways]. It is the judgment of his ways, not of his person, that I am speaking of. Let a man get the best evidence he can for his person; still it is his duty to judge that an evil way will end in destruction. Not to do so is atheism. I do not say that in such a condition a man ought to throw away the evidences of his personal interest in Christ. But I do say that he cannot keep them. There are two ways a man may condemn himself. First, with regard to #emph[desert], when the soul concludes that it deserves to be cast out of the presence of God. This is so far from being a matter of unbelief that it is an effect of faith. Secondly, with regard to the #emph[outcome and event], when the soul concludes that it will be damned. I do not say this is anyone’s duty, nor do I call anyone to it. But this I say: a man ought to conclude that the end of the way he is in is death, so that he may be stirred up to flee from it. And this is another consideration that ought to dwell on such a soul, if it wants to be freed from the entanglement of its lusts.

3\. Consider its #emph[evils]; I mean its #emph[present evils]. Danger looks to what is to come; evil, to what is present. Some of the many evils that come with an unmortified lust may be mentioned:

(1.) It #emph[grieves] the holy and blessed Spirit, who is given to believers to dwell in them and abide with them. So the apostle, urging them away from many lusts and sins (Eph 4:25–29), gives this as the great motive for it in verse 30: “Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you#super[+] were sealed for the day of redemption.” “Do not grieve that Spirit of God,” he says, “by whom you#super[+] receive so many and such great benefits.” He gives one outstanding and comprehensive instance of them: “sealed for the day of redemption.” He is grieved by it. A tender and loving friend is grieved at the unkindness of a friend he has done much for. So it is with this tender and loving Spirit, who has chosen our hearts as a home to dwell in, and there to do for us all that our souls desire. He is grieved when we harbor his enemies, the very ones he is to destroy, in our hearts with him. “He does not willingly afflict or grieve the sons of men” (Lam 3:33); and shall we grieve him daily? This is why he is sometimes said to be “vexed,” and sometimes “grieved in His heart,” to express the deepest sense of our provocation. Now, if there is anything of gracious nobleness of spirit left in the soul, if it is not utterly hardened by the deceitfulness of sin, this consideration will certainly affect it. Consider who and what you are; who the Spirit is who is grieved, what he has done for you, what he comes to your soul about, what he has already done in you; and be ashamed. Among those who walk with God there is no greater motive and spur to holiness in all things, and to keeping their hearts and spirits in all purity and cleanness, than this: the blessed Spirit has undertaken to dwell in them as temples of God, and to keep them fit for him who dwells in them in this way. He is continually considering what they make welcome in their hearts, and he rejoices when his temple is kept undefiled. It made the sin of Zimri far worse that he brought his adulteress into the congregation in the sight of Moses and the rest, who were weeping for the sins of the people (Num 25:6). And is it not a great aggravation of excusing a lust, or allowing it to stay in the heart, when it is entertained (as it must be, if we are believers) under the special eye and view of the Holy Spirit, who takes care to keep his tabernacle pure and holy?

(2.) The #emph[Lord Jesus Christ is wounded afresh] by it. His new creature in the heart is wounded; his love is thwarted; his adversary is gratified. A total abandoning of him through the deceitfulness of sin is “crucifying Him all over again and subjecting Him to open shame.” In the same way, every harboring of the sin he came to destroy wounds and grieves him.

(3.) It will #emph[take away a man’s usefulness] in his generation. His works, his efforts, and his labors seldom receive blessing from God. If he is a preacher, God commonly blights his ministry, so that he labors only to feed the fire and is not honored with any success or with doing any work for God. The same may be said of other callings. The world today is full of poor, withering professing Christians. How few there are who walk in any beauty or glory! How barren, how useless they are for the most part! Among the many reasons that may be given for this sad state, it may rightly be feared that this is not the least effective one: many men harbor spirit-devouring lusts in their bosoms, which lie like worms at the root of their obedience and eat it away and weaken it day by day. All graces, and all the ways and means by which any graces may be exercised and increased, are harmed by this. And as for any success, God blasts such men’s undertakings.

This, then, is my second direction, and it concerns the opposition to be made to lust as it habitually lives in the soul: keep these considerations, or ones like them, of its guilt, danger, and evil alive on your heart. Meditate much on these things. Make your heart dwell and stay on them. Bind your thoughts to these considerations. Do not let them go off or wander from them until they begin to have a powerful influence on your soul, until they make it tremble.
